# C / C++ testing

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic testing rules to GoogleTest
(and Catch2, where a project already uses it) plus sanitizer-backed
verification. Applies to both C and C++ test files.

## Layout and naming

- Tests live under a project's own existing test tree (commonly
  `tests/`, `test/`, or beside the source with a `_test.cpp` suffix) —
  match whichever convention the project already uses, do not invent a
  second one.
- `TEST(SuiteName, CaseName)` for a standalone case; `TEST_F(FixtureName,
  CaseName)` when the case needs shared `SetUp()`/`TearDown()` state via a
  fixture class derived from `::testing::Test`. Name suites/cases for the
  behavior under test, not `Test1`/`Test2`.
- `TEST_P` with `INSTANTIATE_TEST_SUITE_P` for a parameterized case run
  against a range of inputs, instead of copy-pasting the same body with
  different literals across several `TEST`s.

## Fixtures and lifecycle

- Put shared per-test setup in `SetUp()` and teardown in `TearDown()`,
  not the fixture's constructor/destructor, when the setup can fail in a
  way the test should assert on (`SetUp()` runs inside the test's own
  assertion context; a constructor failure does not).
- A fixture that owns a resource (a temp file, a mock server, a thread)
  releases it in `TearDown()` even when the test body fails an assertion
  partway through — do not rely on a `TEST_F` body reaching a cleanup
  statement at its end.

## Assertions

- `ASSERT_*` when the test cannot meaningfully continue after a failure
  (a null pointer that every following line would dereference);
  `EXPECT_*` when later checks in the same test still give useful
  information after one fails.
- `ASSERT_DEATH`/`EXPECT_DEATH` for code whose contract is to terminate
  the process on a given input (an assertion failure, a `CHECK`) — set
  `GTEST_FLAG_SET(death_test_style, "threadsafe")` (or the project's own
  configured style) when the test binary is multi-threaded, since the
  default `fast` style can misbehave by forking a process with more than
  one live thread.
- Compare floating-point values with `EXPECT_NEAR`/`EXPECT_FLOAT_EQ`, not
  `EXPECT_EQ`, unless the value is provably exact (an integer-valued
  computation stored in a float).

## Determinism and concurrency

- Never synchronize a multi-threaded test with `sleep`/`usleep`/
  `std::this_thread::sleep_for` to "give the other thread time" — join
  the thread, wait on a condition variable, or use a future/promise so
  the test is deterministic and not flaky under load.
- A test exercising concurrent code is run under ThreadSanitizer
  (`-fsanitize=thread`) at least once before being trusted, since a data
  race can pass every assertion while still being undefined behavior.

## Sanitizer-backed verification

- Build and run the test suite under AddressSanitizer + UndefinedBehavior-
  Sanitizer for any change touching manual memory management, buffers, or
  pointer arithmetic — a leaking or corrupting test can still report
  green under plain assertions while ASan/UBSan catch the actual bug.
- New behavior gets a new test in the same change; a bug fix (especially
  a memory-safety or UB fix) gets a regression test that reproduces the
  failure under the relevant sanitizer before the fix and passes clean
  after it.

## Fuzzing

- A parser, decoder, or anything else that touches untrusted input
  formats gets a libFuzzer harness (`LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput`, built with
  `-fsanitize=fuzzer,address,undefined`) when the project already has
  fuzzing infrastructure, or a documented seed corpus ready for one to be
  added — do not skip fuzz coverage for a new untrusted-input parser
  solely because unit tests pass.
